  {
    "title": "C. IDs that sort by time",
    "body": "A **time-ordered ID** packs three parts into 64 bits: **41 bits** of milliseconds since a start date (about 69 years), **10 bits** of machine number (up to 1,024 machines) and **12 bits** of counter (4,096 per millisecond). Each machine makes IDs alone, IDs never clash because machine numbers differ, and newer IDs are larger, so new rows land at the end of the index and \"newest first\" is a simple sort. UUID version 7 follows the same idea in 128 bits.",
